//! Cameras for viewing a Y-up, right-handed 3D scene.

/// An opaque camera description.
type t = host

/// Create a camera from an eye position and target with a fixed 45° field of view.
let lookAt : (Vec3.t, Vec3.t) => t
/// Create a first-person camera from position, yaw, pitch, and field of view.
///
/// Zero yaw and pitch look down `+Z`.
///
/// On XR this camera is the authored reference center-eye rig: live head and
/// eye deltas compose in its local basis. Position, orientation, and the near/
/// far planes stay game-owned, while OpenXR owns IPD and per-eye optical FOV.
let firstPerson : (Vec3.t, Angle.t, Angle.t, Angle.t) => t
/// A world-space ray from the camera eye through a logical surface point.
type ray = { origin: Vec3.t, direction: Vec3.t }
/// Map a sampled mouse position through the authored camera.
///
/// Mouse position and extent share one top-left-origin logical coordinate
/// space, so the result stays stable across resize and Retina/device-pixel
/// ratio changes. The direction is normalized and both fields feed directly
/// into `Physics.cast` / `Physics.raycast`. Returns `Option.None` while the
/// pointer is outside the surface or the camera is degenerate.
let toWorldRay : (Input.mouse, t) => Option.t<ray>
/// A tracked pose mapped into world space through an authored camera.
type mappedPose = { position: Input.point3, forward: Input.point3, up: Input.point3 }
/// Map a rig-local tracked pose through the authored camera.
///
/// The returned position, forward, and up vectors are suitable for placing a
/// controller representation or aiming a ray in the authored world.
let mapTrackedPose : (t, Input.pose) => mappedPose
/// A camera at a pose's position, looking along its `forward` and rolled so
/// its `up` points up on screen — for a scope or a camera held in the hand.
///
/// The basis is `firstPerson`'s: forward `+Z` with up `+Y` is yaw = pitch = 0,
/// so a `mapTrackedPose` result (whose tracked `-Z` already maps to authored
/// forward) points this camera where the controller points, roll included.
/// Unlike `firstPerson` it keeps roll and can aim straight up or down.
/// `forward` need not be unit length and `up` need not be perpendicular to it.
/// A zero, non-finite, or parallel `forward`/`up` is a teaching error. Clip
/// planes default to 0.1 / 100; set them with `clip`.
///
/// ```functor
/// let scope = Camera3D.fromPose(aim, Angle.degrees(6.0)) |> Camera3D.clip(1.0, 700.0)
/// ```
let fromPose : (mappedPose, Angle.t) => t

/// Set near and far clipping distances; the camera is last for piping.
///
/// Both distances must be finite with `0 < near < far`; anything else is a
/// teaching error rather than a degenerate projection. Large outdoor worlds
/// should set the far plane explicitly. Keep the near plane as large as
/// gameplay permits to preserve depth-buffer precision.
let clip : (float, float, t) => t

/// Make the camera orthographic, showing `halfHeight` world units above and
/// below its gaze (the width follows the surface's aspect); the camera is
/// last for piping.
///
/// Parallel projection: a part draws the same size at every distance, as in a
/// technical drawing. The eye, gaze and up still come from the camera, and
/// the near/far planes from `clip` (default 0.1 / 100), so back the eye off
/// far enough to keep the scene in front of the near plane; its field of view
/// is unused. `toWorldRay` follows: rays start across the eye's plane, all
/// along the gaze. A headset keeps its own per-eye projection. `halfHeight`
/// must be finite and positive, else a teaching error.
///
/// ```functor
/// let front = Camera3D.lookAt(Vec3.make(0.0, 0.5, -10.0), Vec3.make(0.0, 0.5, 0.0))
///   |> Camera3D.orthographic(0.6)
/// ```
let orthographic : (float, t) => t
